{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>You shall not sacrifice to HaShem your Elohim any flawed thing</b> — a warning against rendering consecrated offerings unfit for the altar (פיגול) by means of an improper utterance. And other interpretations were additionally derived from it regarding the slaughter of consecrated offerings — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 17:1). Now behold, this is a commandment that is expounded, for He already warned concerning one who slaughters animals with blemishes (Leviticus 22:22), and He returned to add the one who renders it unfit (המפגל). And it seems likely that He repeated this warning for the sake of the Israelite who brings the offering, so that he not render it unfit (יפגל) at the time of slaughter; but the priests are diligent and duly warned, and one admonition suffices for them. Therefore He did not repeat the warning regarding a blemish to the offerers." "And the reason for <b>for it is an abomination to HaShem your Elohim</b> — it is this sacrifice, for the one who sacrifices a flawed animal to HaShem thereby despises Him, as stated in the received tradition (Malachi 1:14): \"Cursed be the deceiver… who vows and sacrifices a flawed animal to HaShem, for I am a great King.\" And according to the midrash (Sifrei 147), the abomination applies to one who mates an animal and its mate given as a harlot's fee and the price of a dog, for these are abominations."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:51:03.751855683Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}